    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d137">

                <pb facs="0333" n="313" sameAs="frus:frus1864p2/pg313"/>

                <head>Mr. Adams to Mr. Seward.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 790.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">London,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-09-29"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >September</hi> 29, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> In my despatch No. 787, of the 22d instant,
                    sent last week, I referred to the extraordinary efforts making to procure
                    signatures to an address to the people of the United States in favor of peace. I
                    have since that time received such information as convinces me that it comes
                    from the fruitful source of all similar movements—the rebel emissaries
                    themselves.</p>
                <p>I transmit herewith a printed copy of the paper, as it was originally prepared,
                    (No. 1,) and likewise of the corrected proof, as it was finally issued, (No. 2.)
                    Both bear, as well in the omissions as in the substitutions, decided ear-marks
                    of their origin.</p>
                <p>Our vigilant British friends in Manchester, needing no prompting whatever from
                    American sources, promptly issued and circulated a paper in counteraction of the
                    movement—a copy of which is also transmitted, (No. 3.)</p>
                <p>I have the honor to be, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>CHARLES FRANCIS ADAMS.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>Hon. <hi rendition="#small-caps">William H. Seward</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Secretary of State, Washington, D. C.</hi></p>
                </postscript>





